    id: "instruction-set", title: "Instruction Set Architecture (ISA)", difficulty: "hard",
    definition: "The ISA is the programmer-visible interface to the CPU — the set of instructions the hardware can execute. It defines opcodes (operations: ADD, MOV, JMP, LOAD, STORE), data types, registers, addressing modes, and memory model. The ISA is the contract between hardware and software: compilers target it, CPUs implement it. RISC ISAs (ARM, MIPS) have few, simple, fixed-length instructions. CISC ISAs (x86) have many, complex, variable-length instructions. The ISA is the architecture; the circuit design is the organization.",
    coreUnderstanding: "The ISA (Instruction Set Architecture) is the programmer-visible interface to the CPU — the set of instructions the hardware can execute. It defines: opcodes (operations like ADD, MOV, JMP, LOAD, STORE), data types (int, float), registers (how many, what size), addressing modes (how operands are specified), and the memory model. The ISA is the contract between hardware and software: compilers target it, CPUs implement it. RISC ISAs (ARM, MIPS, RISC-V) have few, simple, fixed-length instructions — easy to decode, fast pipelining. CISC ISAs (x86) have many, complex, variable-length instructions — more work per instruction, but harder to decode and pipeline. The ISA is the architecture; the circuit design is the organization. The same ISA (x86) can be implemented differently (Intel vs AMD).",
    howItWorks: "1. Opcodes: the operation to perform. ADD, SUB, MUL, DIV, AND, OR, MOV, JMP, LOAD, STORE, CMP.\n2. Data types: int (8/16/32/64-bit), float (32/64-bit), char (8-bit). The ISA defines what types are supported.\n3. Registers: how many, what size. ARM: 16 registers (32/64-bit). x86: 16 general-purpose (64-bit).\n4. Addressing modes: how operands are specified. Immediate, register, direct, indirect, indexed.\n5. Memory model: how memory is addressed. Byte-addressable, little-endian or big-endian.\n6. Instruction format: how the bits are laid out. RISC: fixed-length (4 bytes). CISC: variable-length (1-15 bytes for x86).\n7. RISC vs CISC: RISC (ARM, MIPS) = few, simple, fixed-length. CISC (x86) = many, complex, variable-length.\n8. The ISA is the contract: compilers target it, CPUs implement it. The same ISA can be implemented differently.",
    codeExamples: [{ lang: "text", code: "-- ISA defines the programmer-visible interface:\n\n-- Opcodes (operations):\n-- ADD R1, R2, R3   (R1 = R2 + R3)\n-- SUB R1, R2, R3   (R1 = R2 - R3)\n-- MOV R1, R2       (R1 = R2)\n-- LOAD R1, [R2]    (R1 = memory[R2])\n-- STORE [R1], R2   (memory[R1] = R2)\n-- JMP 1000         (PC = 1000, unconditional jump)\n-- BEQ R1, R2, 1000 (if R1 == R2, PC = 1000, branch if equal)\n-- CMP R1, R2       (set flags based on R1 - R2)\n\n-- Data types:\n-- int: 8-bit (byte), 16-bit (halfword), 32-bit (word), 64-bit (doubleword)\n-- float: 32-bit (single), 64-bit (double)\n-- char: 8-bit (ASCII character)\n\n-- Registers:\n-- ARM: 16 registers (R0-R15), 32-bit or 64-bit\n-- x86: 16 general-purpose (RAX, RBX, ...), 64-bit\n-- MIPS: 32 registers (R0-R31), 32-bit or 64-bit\n\n-- Addressing modes:\n-- Immediate: ADD R1, R2, #5    (operand is a constant)\n-- Register:  ADD R1, R2, R3    (operand is in a register)\n-- Direct:    LOAD R1, [1000]   (operand is at memory address 1000)\n-- Indirect:  LOAD R1, [R2]     (address is in R2)\n-- Indexed:   LOAD R1, [R2+4]   (address = R2 + 4)\n\n-- Instruction format:\n-- RISC (ARM, fixed 4 bytes):\n--   [opcode(8)][Rd(4)][Rn(4)][Rm(4)][shift(5)][constant(7)]\n--   Easy to decode, fast pipelining.\n\n-- CISC (x86, variable 1-15 bytes):\n--   [prefixes(0-4)][opcode(1-3)][ModR/M(1)][SIB(1)][displacement(0-4)][immediate(0-4)]\n--   Hard to decode, but more work per instruction.\n\n-- RISC vs CISC:\n-- RISC (ARM, MIPS, RISC-V):\n--   - Few, simple, fixed-length instructions\n--   - Load/store architecture (only LOAD/STORE access memory)\n--   - Many registers\n--   - Compiler does the work\n\n-- CISC (x86):\n--   - Many, complex, variable-length instructions\n--   - Memory-to-memory operations\n--   - Fewer registers\n--   - Hardware does the work", output: "-- ISA: opcodes, data types, registers, addressing modes, memory model.\n-- RISC: few, simple, fixed-length (ARM, MIPS).\n-- CISC: many, complex, variable-length (x86).\n-- The contract between hardware and software.", note: "The ISA is the programmer-visible interface: opcodes, data types, registers, addressing modes. RISC (ARM, MIPS) = few, simple, fixed-length. CISC (x86) = many, complex, variable-length." }],
    marginNotes: ["The ISA is the contract between hardware and software: compilers target it, CPUs implement it. The same ISA (x86) can be implemented differently (Intel vs AMD).", "RISC (ARM, MIPS) has few, simple, fixed-length instructions — easy to decode, fast pipelining. CISC (x86) has many, complex, variable-length instructions — more work per instruction but harder to decode.", "The ISA is the architecture (what the programmer sees); the circuit design is the organization (how it's built). The same architecture can have different organizations."],
