    id: "addressing-modes", title: "Addressing Modes", difficulty: "medium",
    definition: "Addressing modes define how an instruction specifies its operand. Immediate (operand is a constant in the instruction: ADD R1, #5). Register (operand is in a register: ADD R1, R2). Direct/Absolute (operand is at a memory address in the instruction: LOAD R1, [1000]). Register Indirect (address is in a register: LOAD R1, [R2]). Indexed (base + offset: LOAD R1, [R2+4]). Indirect (address of address). More modes = more flexibility but more complex hardware.",
    coreUnderstanding: "Addressing modes define how an instruction specifies its operand (the data it operates on). The common modes: Immediate (the operand is a constant embedded in the instruction — ADD R1, #5 adds 5 to R1), Register (the operand is in a register — ADD R1, R2 adds R2 to R1), Direct/Absolute (the operand is at a memory address specified in the instruction — LOAD R1, [1000] loads from address 1000), Register Indirect (the address is in a register — LOAD R1, [R2] loads from the address in R2), Indexed (base + offset — LOAD R1, [R2+4] loads from R2+4, used for arrays and structs). More addressing modes = more flexibility for the programmer but more complex hardware to decode. RISC ISAs have fewer modes (simpler hardware); CISC has more (more flexibility).",
    howItWorks: "1. Immediate: operand is a constant in the instruction. ADD R1, #5 → R1 = R1 + 5. Fast (no memory access), but limited to small constants.\n2. Register: operand is in a register. ADD R1, R2 → R1 = R1 + R2. Fastest (no memory access).\n3. Direct/Absolute: operand is at a memory address in the instruction. LOAD R1, [1000] → R1 = memory[1000]. Slow (memory access), but simple.\n4. Register Indirect: address is in a register. LOAD R1, [R2] → R1 = memory[R2]. Used for pointers.\n5. Indexed (Base + Offset): address = base register + offset. LOAD R1, [R2+4] → R1 = memory[R2+4]. Used for arrays and structs.\n6. Indirect (address of address): the instruction contains an address, which contains the operand address. Rare.\n7. More modes = more flexibility but more complex hardware. RISC has fewer (simpler); CISC has more (more powerful).",
    codeExamples: [{ lang: "text", code: "-- Addressing modes (how an instruction specifies its operand):\n\n-- 1. Immediate (constant in the instruction):\n--    ADD R1, #5        → R1 = R1 + 5\n--    MOV R2, #100      → R2 = 100\n--    Fast (no memory access), but limited to small constants.\n\n-- 2. Register (operand is in a register):\n--    ADD R1, R2        → R1 = R1 + R2\n--    MOV R1, R2        → R1 = R2\n--    Fastest (no memory access, register is in the CPU).\n\n-- 3. Direct/Absolute (memory address in the instruction):\n--    LOAD R1, [1000]   → R1 = memory[1000]\n--    STORE [1000], R1  → memory[1000] = R1\n--    Slow (memory access), but simple.\n\n-- 4. Register Indirect (address is in a register):\n--    LOAD R1, [R2]     → R1 = memory[R2]\n--    STORE [R2], R1    → memory[R2] = R1\n--    Used for pointers (R2 points to the data).\n\n-- 5. Indexed (base + offset):\n--    LOAD R1, [R2+4]   → R1 = memory[R2 + 4]\n--    LOAD R1, [R2+R3]  → R1 = memory[R2 + R3]\n--    Used for arrays (R2 = base, offset = index × element size)\n--    and structs (R2 = base, offset = field offset).\n\n-- 6. Indirect (address of address):\n--    LOAD R1, [[1000]] → R1 = memory[memory[1000]]\n--    Rare, complex. The instruction contains an address,\n--    which contains the operand address.\n\n-- RISC vs CISC addressing modes:\n-- RISC (ARM, MIPS): fewer modes (immediate, register, indexed)\n--   - Simpler hardware, faster decode\n-- CISC (x86): many modes (immediate, register, direct, indirect,\n--   indexed, scaled, etc.)\n--   - More flexibility, but more complex hardware\n\n-- Example: array access (indexed addressing)\n-- int arr[10];\n-- arr[3] = 42;\n-- Assembly:\n--   MOV R1, #arr      (R1 = base address of arr)\n--   MOV R2, #3        (R2 = index)\n--   MOV R3, #42       (R3 = value)\n--   STR R3, [R1, R2, LSL #2]  (memory[R1 + R2*4] = R3)\n--   (LSL #2 = shift left 2 = multiply by 4, for 4-byte ints)", output: "-- Immediate: constant in instruction.\n-- Register: operand in register.\n-- Direct: memory address in instruction.\n-- Register Indirect: address in register.\n-- Indexed: base + offset (arrays, structs).\n-- More modes = more flexibility, more complex hardware.", note: "Addressing modes define how operands are specified: immediate (constant), register, direct (address), indirect (pointer), indexed (base+offset for arrays/structs). More modes = more flexibility but more complex hardware." }],
    marginNotes: ["Indexed addressing (base + offset) is used for arrays and structs. LOAD R1, [R2+4] loads from R2+4 — R2 is the base, 4 is the offset.", "RISC has fewer addressing modes (simpler hardware, faster decode). CISC has more (more flexibility, but more complex hardware).", "Immediate addressing is fast (no memory access) but limited to small constants. Register addressing is the fastest (no memory access, register is in the CPU)."],
