  {
    id: "parallel-processing", title: "Parallel Processing & Multi-core", difficulty: "hard",
    definition: "Parallel processing executes multiple instructions simultaneously. Instruction-level parallelism (ILP): pipelining, superscalar (multiple execution units), out-of-order execution, VLIW. Thread-level parallelism (TLP): multi-core (multiple CPUs on one chip), hyper-threading/SMT (one core executes multiple threads, sharing resources). Data-level parallelism (DLP): SIMD (one instruction operates on multiple data — SSE, AVX), vector processors, GPUs. Flynn's taxonomy: SISD, SIMD, MISD, MIMD. Power and thermal limits (dark silicon) drove the shift from higher clock speeds to more cores.",
    coreUnderstanding: "Parallel processing executes multiple instructions simultaneously to improve performance. There are three levels. Instruction-level parallelism (ILP): pipelining (overlap stages), superscalar (multiple execution units — execute multiple instructions per cycle), out-of-order execution (reorder instructions for better parallelism), VLIW (Very Long Instruction Word — the compiler packages multiple instructions). Thread-level parallelism (TLP): multi-core (multiple CPUs on one chip), hyper-threading/SMT (Simultaneous Multi-Threading — one core executes multiple threads, sharing resources). Data-level parallelism (DLP): SIMD (Single Instruction Multiple Data — one instruction operates on multiple data, e.g., SSE, AVX for vector operations), vector processors, GPUs. Flynn's taxonomy classifies architectures: SISD (single instruction, single data — traditional), SIMD (single instruction, multiple data — vector), MISD (rare), MIMD (multiple instruction, multiple data — multi-core). Power and thermal limits (dark silicon — can't power all transistors simultaneously) drove the shift from higher clock speeds to more cores.",
    howItWorks: "1. Instruction-Level Parallelism (ILP):\n   - Pipelining: overlap instruction stages (fetch, decode, execute, ...).\n   - Superscalar: multiple execution units (execute 2-4 instructions per cycle).\n   - Out-of-order execution: reorder instructions for better parallelism (avoid stalls).\n   - VLIW (Very Long Instruction Word): the compiler packages multiple instructions into one wide word.\n2. Thread-Level Parallelism (TLP):\n   - Multi-core: multiple CPUs (cores) on one chip. Each core runs a separate thread.\n   - Hyper-threading/SMT (Simultaneous Multi-Threading): one core executes multiple threads, sharing resources (ALU, cache). Appears as 2 logical cores per physical core.\n3. Data-Level Parallelism (DLP):\n   - SIMD (Single Instruction Multiple Data): one instruction operates on multiple data elements.\n   - SSE, AVX (x86): vector instructions (operate on 4-16 elements at once).\n   - GPUs: thousands of cores for massive data parallelism (graphics, ML).\n4. Flynn's Taxonomy:\n   - SISD (Single Instruction, Single Data): traditional scalar CPU.\n   - SIMD (Single Instruction, Multiple Data): vector processors, SSE/AVX, GPUs.\n   - MISD (Multiple Instruction, Single Data): rare (fault-tolerant systems).\n   - MIMD (Multiple Instruction, Multiple Data): multi-core, distributed systems.\n5. Dark silicon: transistors are so dense that you can't power them all simultaneously (thermal limits). This drove the shift from higher clock speeds to more cores.",
    codeExamples: [{ lang: "text", code: "-- Parallel Processing (execute multiple instructions simultaneously):\n\n-- 1. Instruction-Level Parallelism (ILP):\n--    Pipelining: overlap stages (F D E M W for multiple instructions).\n--    Superscalar: multiple execution units (2-4 instructions per cycle).\n--      Cycle 1: I1-F, I2-F (two fetches simultaneously)\n--      Cycle 2: I1-D, I2-D, I3-F, I4-F (two decodes, two fetches)\n--    Out-of-order: reorder instructions to avoid stalls.\n--      ADD R1, R2, R3  (depends on R2, R3)\n--      MUL R4, R5, R6  (independent — can execute before ADD if ready)\n--    VLIW: compiler packages multiple instructions into one wide word.\n\n-- 2. Thread-Level Parallelism (TLP):\n--    Multi-core: multiple CPUs on one chip.\n--      4-core CPU: 4 threads running simultaneously.\n--      8-core, 16-core, 64-core: more parallelism.\n--    Hyper-threading/SMT: one core executes multiple threads.\n--      1 physical core → 2 logical cores (shares ALU, cache).\n--      When one thread stalls (cache miss), the other runs.\n--      ~30% performance improvement (not 2x — resources are shared).\n\n-- 3. Data-Level Parallelism (DLP):\n--    SIMD (Single Instruction Multiple Data):\n--      One instruction operates on multiple data elements.\n--      Example: ADD 4 pairs of numbers with one instruction.\n--        AVX: ADD [A0,A1,A2,A3,A4,A5,A6,A7] + [B0,B1,B2,B3,B4,B5,B6,B7]\n--        → [C0,C1,C2,C3,C4,C5,C6,C7] (8 additions, 1 instruction)\n--    SSE, AVX (x86): 128-bit, 256-bit, 512-bit vector instructions.\n--    GPUs: thousands of cores for massive data parallelism.\n--      Graphics: process millions of pixels simultaneously.\n--      Machine learning: matrix operations (thousands of parallel multiplies).\n\n-- 4. Flynn's Taxonomy:\n--    SISD (Single Instruction, Single Data): traditional scalar CPU.\n--      One instruction, one data element at a time.\n--    SIMD (Single Instruction, Multiple Data): vector, SSE/AVX, GPUs.\n--      One instruction, multiple data elements (vector).\n--    MISD (Multiple Instruction, Single Data): rare (fault-tolerant).\n--      Multiple instructions on the same data (redundancy).\n--    MIMD (Multiple Instruction, Multiple Data): multi-core, distributed.\n--      Multiple instructions, multiple data (independent cores).\n\n-- 5. Dark Silicon:\n--    Transistors are so dense that you can't power them all simultaneously.\n--    Thermal limits: the chip would overheat if all transistors were active.\n--    This drove the shift from higher clock speeds (more heat) to more cores\n--    (parallelism without more heat per core).\n--    Result: multi-core CPUs (2, 4, 8, 16, 64 cores) instead of 10 GHz single-core.", output: "-- ILP: pipelining, superscalar, out-of-order, VLIW.\n-- TLP: multi-core, hyper-threading/SMT.\n-- DLP: SIMD (SSE, AVX), GPUs.\n-- Flynn: SISD, SIMD, MISD, MIMD.\n-- Dark silicon drove the shift to multi-core.", note: "Parallel processing: ILP (pipelining, superscalar), TLP (multi-core, hyper-threading), DLP (SIMD, GPUs). Flynn's taxonomy: SISD, SIMD, MISD, MIMD. Dark silicon drove the shift from clock speed to multi-core." }],
    marginNotes: ["Dark silicon drove the shift from higher clock speeds to more cores — transistors are so dense that you can't power them all (thermal limits). Multi-core gives parallelism without more heat per core.", "Hyper-threading/SMT gives ~30% improvement (not 2x) because resources are shared. When one thread stalls (cache miss), the other runs. It's not a full second core.", "GPUs are massive SIMD machines — thousands of cores for data parallelism (graphics, ML). CPUs have a few powerful cores; GPUs have thousands of simple cores."],
    commonMistakes: ["Confusing ILP (instruction-level: pipelining, superscalar) with TLP (thread-level: multi-core) and DLP (data-level: SIMD). They're different levels of parallelism.", "Thinking hyper-threading doubles performance — it gives ~30% because resources are shared. It's not a full second core.", "Not understanding dark silicon — transistors are so dense that you can't power them all (thermal limits). This is why we have multi-core instead of 10 GHz single-core."],
    relatedTopics: ["Pipelining", "RISC vs CISC", "CPU Organization"],
    externalLinks: [{ label: "Parallel Processing — GeeksforGeeks", url: "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/parallel-processing-in-computer-architecture/" }, { label: "Flynn's Taxonomy — Wikipedia", url: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flynn%27s_taxonomy" }],
    mcqs: [
      { question: "What are the three levels of parallelism?", options: ["Fetch, decode, execute", "Instruction-level (ILP), thread-level (TLP), data-level (DLP)", "L1, L2, L3", "Read, write, compute"], answer: 1, explanations: ["Those are pipeline stages.", "Correct — ILP (pipelining, superscalar), TLP (multi-core, hyper-threading), DLP (SIMD, GPUs).", "Those are cache levels.", "Those are operations."], difficulty: "hard" },
      { question: "What is hyper-threading/SMT?", options: ["Multiple cores on one chip", "One core executes multiple threads, sharing resources (~30% improvement, not 2x)", "A type of cache", "A type of bus"], answer: 1, explanations: ["That's multi-core.", "Correct — hyper-threading/SMT: one physical core executes multiple threads, sharing resources (ALU, cache). ~30% improvement (not 2x, because resources are shared).", "Not a cache.", "Not a bus."], difficulty: "hard" },
      { question: "What drove the shift from higher clock speeds to more cores?", options: ["Cost", "Dark silicon — thermal limits (can't power all transistors simultaneously)", "Power consumption", "Marketing"], answer: 1, explanations: ["Not about cost.", "Correct — dark silicon: transistors are so dense that you can't power them all (thermal limits). This drove the shift from higher clock speeds (more heat) to more cores (parallelism without more heat per core).", "Power is related but dark silicon is the specific term.", "Not marketing."], difficulty: "hard" }
    ]
  },
  {
    id: "error-correcting-codes", title: "Error Correcting Codes (ECC)", difficulty: "hard",
    definition: "Memory and data transmission can flip bits due to noise or radiation. Parity bits detect single-bit errors but can't correct them. Hamming codes add redundant bits to detect AND correct single-bit errors (SEC — Single Error Correction) and detect double-bit errors (DED — Double Error Detection). ECC memory uses SEC-DED to protect servers from silent data corruption. CRC detects errors in storage and networking. Reed-Solomon codes correct burst errors on CDs/DVDs. More redundancy = more reliability but more overhead.",
    coreUnderstanding: "Memory and data transmission can flip bits due to noise, interference, or cosmic radiation (especially at high altitudes or in space). Parity bits detect single-bit errors but can't correct them (you know an error occurred, but not which bit). Hamming codes add redundant bits to detect AND correct single-bit errors (SEC — Single Error Correction) and detect double-bit errors (DED — Double Error Detection). ECC memory uses SEC-DED to protect servers from silent data corruption (a flipped bit in a database could corrupt data silently). CRC (Cyclic Redundancy Check) detects errors in storage and networking (but doesn't correct — requests retransmission). Reed-Solomon codes correct burst errors (multiple consecutive bits) on CDs/DVDs and QR codes. The trade-off: more redundancy = more reliability but more overhead (extra bits, storage, bandwidth).",
    howItWorks: "1. Parity bit: add 1 bit to make the total number of 1s even (or odd). Detects single-bit errors. Can't correct (you know an error occurred, but not which bit).\n2. Hamming code: add redundant bits at positions that are powers of 2 (1, 2, 4, 8, ...). Each redundant bit checks specific data bits. Can detect AND correct single-bit errors (SEC). Can detect double-bit errors (DED).\n3. SEC-DED (Single Error Correction, Double Error Detection): the standard for ECC memory. Corrects single-bit errors, detects double-bit errors (which require retransmission or replacement).\n4. ECC memory: uses SEC-DED to protect servers. Detects and corrects single-bit errors silently. Detects double-bit errors (which are reported and may cause a shutdown to prevent corruption).\n5. CRC (Cyclic Redundancy Check): detects errors in storage and networking. Detects burst errors. Doesn't correct — requests retransmission (ARQ).\n6. Reed-Solomon: corrects burst errors (multiple consecutive bits). Used in CDs/DVDs (scratches), QR codes, deep-space communication.\n7. Trade-off: more redundancy = more reliability but more overhead (extra bits, storage, bandwidth).",
    codeExamples: [{ lang: "text", code: "-- Error Correcting Codes:\n\n-- 1. Parity bit (detect single-bit errors, can't correct):\n--    Data: 1011001 (four 1s, even parity)\n--    Parity bit: 0 (to keep even number of 1s)\n--    Transmitted: 10110010\n--    If one bit flips: 10110110 (five 1s, odd) → error detected.\n--    But: which bit flipped? Can't tell. Can't correct.\n--    If two bits flip: 10111110 (six 1s, even) → error NOT detected.\n\n-- 2. Hamming code (detect AND correct single-bit errors):\n--    Data: 1011 (4 bits)\n--    Add parity bits at positions 1, 2, 4 (powers of 2):\n--    Position: 1  2  3  4  5  6  7\n--    Content: P1 P2 D1 P4 D2 D3 D4\n--    P1 checks positions 1, 3, 5, 7 (P1, D1, D2, D4)\n--    P2 checks positions 2, 3, 6, 7 (P2, D1, D3, D4)\n--    P4 checks positions 4, 5, 6, 7 (P4, D2, D3, D4)\n--    If a bit flips, the parity checks identify the position → correct it.\n--    Example: position 5 flips → P1 and P4 fail → 101 → position 5 → correct.\n\n-- 3. SEC-DED (Single Error Correction, Double Error Detection):\n--    Standard for ECC memory.\n--    Corrects single-bit errors (silently — the system continues).\n--    Detects double-bit errors (reports — may cause shutdown to prevent corruption).\n--    Used in servers for reliability.\n\n-- 4. ECC Memory:\n--    Uses SEC-DED to protect servers from silent data corruption.\n--    A flipped bit in a database could corrupt data silently.\n--    ECC detects and corrects single-bit errors (no data loss).\n--    ECC detects double-bit errors (reports, may shut down).\n--    Non-ECC (consumer RAM): no error correction. Cheaper.\n\n-- 5. CRC (Cyclic Redundancy Check):\n--    Detects errors in storage and networking.\n--    Detects burst errors (consecutive bit flips).\n--    Doesn't correct — requests retransmission (ARQ).\n--    Used in: Ethernet, Wi-Fi, hard drives, USB.\n\n-- 6. Reed-Solomon:\n--    Corrects burst errors (multiple consecutive bits).\n--    Used in: CDs/DVDs (scratches cause burst errors),\n--             QR codes, deep-space communication.\n--    More redundancy than Hamming but handles burst errors.\n\n-- Trade-off:\n-- More redundancy = more reliability but more overhead.\n-- Parity: 1 bit overhead, detect only.\n-- Hamming: ~log(n) bits overhead, detect + correct single.\n-- SEC-DED: ~log(n)+1 bits, correct single + detect double.\n-- Reed-Solomon: more overhead, correct burst errors.", output: "-- Parity: detect single-bit, can't correct.\n-- Hamming: detect AND correct single-bit (SEC), detect double (DED).\n-- ECC memory: SEC-DED for servers.\n-- CRC: detect (no correct) in storage/networking.\n-- Reed-Solomon: correct burst errors (CDs, QR codes).\n-- More redundancy = more reliability, more overhead.", note: "Error correcting codes: parity (detect only), Hamming (correct single-bit, SEC-DED), ECC memory (servers), CRC (detect, no correct), Reed-Solomon (burst errors). More redundancy = more reliability but more overhead." }],
    marginNotes: ["ECC memory uses SEC-DED (Single Error Correction, Double Error Detection) to protect servers from silent data corruption. Corrects single-bit errors silently; detects double-bit errors.", "Hamming codes add redundant bits at positions that are powers of 2. Each redundant bit checks specific data bits. This allows detecting AND correcting single-bit errors.", "Reed-Solomon codes correct burst errors (multiple consecutive bits) — used in CDs/DVDs (scratches), QR codes, deep-space communication. More overhead than Hamming but handles bursts."],
    commonMistakes: ["Confusing error detection (find errors, request retransmission) with error correction (fix errors without retransmission). Parity detects; Hamming corrects.", "Forgetting that ECC memory is for servers — consumer RAM is non-ECC (no error correction). ECC prevents silent data corruption in servers.", "Not understanding that Reed-Solomon corrects burst errors (multiple consecutive bits) — used in CDs/DVDs (scratches), QR codes. Hamming corrects single-bit errors."],
    relatedTopics: ["Main Memory (RAM)", "Error Detection & Correction", "Secondary Storage: SSD & HDD"],
    externalLinks: [{ label: "ECC — GeeksforGeeks", url: "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/error-correcting-codes/" }, { label: "Hamming Code — Wikipedia", url: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hamming_code" }],
    mcqs: [
      { question: "What can a parity bit do?", options: ["Correct single-bit errors", "Detect single-bit errors (but can't correct)", "Correct burst errors", "Detect and correct"], answer: 1, explanations: ["Parity can't correct.", "Correct — parity detects single-bit errors (the parity changes) but can't correct (you don't know which bit flipped).", "Parity can't correct burst errors.", "Parity only detects."], difficulty: "medium" },
      { question: "What does ECC memory use for error correction?", options: ["Parity", "SEC-DED (Single Error Correction, Double Error Detection)", "CRC", "Reed-Solomon"], answer: 1, explanations: ["Parity only detects.", "Correct — ECC memory uses SEC-DED: corrects single-bit errors (silently), detects double-bit errors (reports). Protects servers from silent data corruption.", "CRC detects but doesn't correct.", "Reed-Solomon is for burst errors (CDs)."], difficulty: "hard" },
      { question: "Which code corrects burst errors (used in CDs/DVDs)?", options: ["Parity", "Hamming", "CRC", "Reed-Solomon"], answer: 3, explanations: ["Parity detects only.", "Hamming corrects single-bit.", "CRC detects only.", "Correct — Reed-Solomon corrects burst errors (multiple consecutive bits). Used in CDs/DVDs (scratches), QR codes, deep-space communication."], difficulty: "hard" }
    ]
  }
